#Problem 3
```{r}
unzip(
  "data/data_mci.zip",
  exdir = "data/data_mci"
)
```
```{r}
list.files("data/data_mci/data_mci")
```
```{r}
mci_baseline =
  read_csv(
    "data/data_mci/data_mci/MCI_baseline.csv",
    skip = 1,
    na = "."
  ) |>
  clean_names() |>
  mutate(
    sex = case_match(
      sex,
      0 ~ "Female",
      1 ~ "Male"
    ),
    apoe4 = case_match(
      apoe4,
      0 ~ "Non-carrier",
      1 ~ "Carrier"
    )
  ) |>
  filter(
    is.na(age_at_onset) | age_at_onset > current_age
  )

```
```{r}
baseline_summary =
  mci_baseline |>
  summarize(
    participants_recruited = n(),
    developed_mci = sum(!is.na(age_at_onset)),
    average_baseline_age = mean(current_age),
    female_apoe4_carrier_prop =
      mean(apoe4[sex == "Female"] == "Carrier")
  )

baseline_summary
```
After cleaning the baseline demographic data and removing participants who had already developed MCI by the study baseline, 479 participants remained in the study. Among these participants, 93 developed MCI during follow-up. The average baseline age was approximately 65.03 years. Among women in the study, 30.0% were APOE4 carriers. The `sex` and `apoe4` variables were recoded from numeric indicators into meaningful categorical variables, and missing values were coded as `NA`.
```{r}
mci_amyloid =
  read_csv(
    "data/data_mci/data_mci/mci_amyloid.csv",
    skip = 1,
    na = c(".", "NA", "Na")
  ) |>
  clean_names()

```
```{r}
mci_amyloid_long =
  mci_amyloid |>
  pivot_longer(
    cols = baseline:time_8,
    names_to = "visit",
    values_to = "amyloid"
  ) |>
  mutate(
    visit = case_match(
      visit,
      "baseline" ~ 0,
      "time_2" ~ 2,
      "time_4" ~ 4,
      "time_6" ~ 6,
      "time_8" ~ 8
    ),
    amyloid = as.numeric(amyloid)
  )

```
The amyloid biomarker dataset was imported by skipping the first descriptive row so that the actual variable names were used as column names. Missing values were coded as `NA`, and variable names were standardized. The original dataset was stored in wide format, with separate columns for biomarker measurements at baseline and at 2-, 4-, 6-, and 8-year follow-up visits. I reshaped the data into long format so that each row represents one participant at one visit, with separate variables for participant ID, visit time, and amyloid 42/40 ratio.
```{r}
baseline_only =
  mci_baseline |>
  anti_join(
    mci_amyloid,
    by = c("id" = "study_id")
  )

amyloid_only =
  mci_amyloid |>
  anti_join(
    mci_baseline,
    by = c("study_id" = "id")
  )

baseline_only
amyloid_only
```
There were 8 participants who appeared only in the baseline demographic dataset and 16 participants who appeared only in the amyloid dataset. Therefore, the two datasets do not contain exactly the same set of participants, and participants not represented in both datasets will be excluded when the datasets are combined.
```{r}
mci_combined =
  mci_baseline |>
  inner_join(
    mci_amyloid_long,
    by = c("id" = "study_id")
  )

```
The combined demographic and amyloid dataset contains 2,355 observations and 8 variables. Only participants who appeared in both the cleaned baseline demographic dataset and the amyloid dataset were retained. Each participant can contribute multiple observations corresponding to biomarker measurements at baseline and follow-up visits.
```{r}
write_csv(
  mci_combined,
  "data/mci_combined.csv"
)
```



